5. SOLID Requirements

Single Responsibility

Separate:

Configuration loading
Configuration validation
Configuration compilation
XML parsing
Path matching
Record lifecycle
Value conversion
Transformation
Error handling
Metrics

Do not create a giant "XmlFlattenService".

---

Open/Closed Principle

Adding a new transformation must NOT require modification of the flattening engine.

Use:

public interface Transformer {

    Object transform(
        Object value,
        TransformationContext context
    );
}

Example:

TRIM
UPPERCASE
LOWERCASE
DEFAULT
DATE_FORMAT
DECIMAL_SCALE
CONCAT

---

Liskov Substitution

Implementations of parser, transformer and output components must be interchangeable through interfaces.

---

Interface Segregation

Prefer small interfaces:

FlattenEngine
RecordConsumer
Transformer
ValueConverter
PathMatcher
ConfigurationLoader

Avoid large interfaces.

---

Dependency Inversion

The core must depend on abstractions.

Do not make the domain directly depend on:

Woodstox
Spring
Jackson
Kafka

where avoidable.

---

6. XML Parsing

Use streaming XML processing.

Preferred implementation:

StAX
+
Woodstox

Do NOT use:

DOM

for the main flattening path.

Avoid:

DocumentBuilder

and:

XPath.evaluate()

for every field.

Compile configured paths once and match XML events while streaming.

---

7. XML Security

The XML parser MUST be hardened.

Disable:

DTD processing
External entities
External DTDs
External schemas where not explicitly required

Protect against:

XXE
XML entity expansion
Billion Laughs
External resource access

The engine must never fetch arbitrary external resources while processing XML.

Add security-focused unit tests.

---

8. Configuration Model

Create immutable configuration classes.

Suggested model:

public record FlattenConfig(
    String name,
    MessageDefinition message,
    RecordDefinition record,
    List<FieldDefinition> fields
) {}

Use immutable records/classes wherever practical.

Example:

public record FieldDefinition(
    String name,
    String path,
    DataType type,
    List<String> firstAvailable,
    Object defaultValue,
    List<String> transformations
) {}

Do not expose mutable internal collections.

---

9. Configuration Compilation

Never parse YAML and resolve paths for every XML.

The lifecycle must be:

YAML
  │
  ▼
Configuration Loader
  │
  ▼
Validation
  │
  ▼
Namespace Resolution
  │
  ▼
Path Compilation
  │
  ▼
Transformation Compilation
  │
  ▼
CompiledMapping
  │
  ▼
Reusable FlattenEngine

Configuration should normally be loaded and compiled once during application startup.

---

10. Path Engine

Do not implement a complete XPath engine for V1.

Implement a controlled path expression language.

Support:

A/B/C
A/B/@attribute
../Parent
absolute paths
relative paths

Prefer compiled path tokens:

public final class CompiledPath {

    private final PathToken[] tokens;

}

Do not repeatedly split strings during XML processing.

The hot path should operate on precompiled structures.

---

11. Repeating Record Model

The most important concept is the record root.

Example:

record:
  path: /Document/CstmrCdtTrfInitn/PmtInf/CdtTrfTxInf

If the XML contains:

CdtTrfTxInf #1
CdtTrfTxInf #2
CdtTrfTxInf #3

emit:

FlatRecord #1
FlatRecord #2
FlatRecord #3

Do not create a list containing all records.

Use:

XML → Record → Consumer → discard/reuse → next Record

---

12. Parent Context

Support inherited values.

Example:

GrpHdr
   MsgId = ABC

PmtInf
   PmtInfId = P001

   CdtTrfTxInf
      TxId = T001

   CdtTrfTxInf
      TxId = T002

Output:

MsgId | PmtInfId | TxId
ABC   | P001     | T001
ABC   | P001     | T002

The engine must maintain only the necessary active context.

Do NOT retain the entire XML tree.

---

13. Memory Requirements

The implementation must have memory complexity approximately:

O(depth + configured-fields)

rather than:

O(XML-size)

Avoid:

List<FlatRecord>

for the complete document.

Avoid:

String xml = inputStream.readAllBytes()

Avoid:

byte[] entireDocument

Avoid retaining parser events.

---

14. FlatRecord Design

Do not use:

Map<String, Object>

as the primary internal representation if it causes unnecessary allocation.

Prefer:

public final class FlatRecord {

    private final Object[] values;

}

with:

ColumnDefinition[]

metadata.

Expose convenient name-based access externally if required.

The hot path should be index-based.

---

