18. Thread Safety

The compiled configuration and engine should be reusable across threads if designed as immutable.

Do NOT maintain:

static mutable HashMap
static ThreadLocal without justification
shared StringBuilder
shared mutable parser
shared mutable RecordContext

Each XML processing invocation must have its own processing state.

Parser instances must not be shared between concurrent operations unless the implementation explicitly guarantees thread safety.

---

19. Multi-Pod Kubernetes Safety

The library itself must be stateless.

Do not depend on:

local filesystem state
in-memory distributed state
pod identity
static counters
local locks

Every request must be independently processable.

If configuration is loaded at startup:

Pod A → compiled config
Pod B → compiled config
Pod C → compiled config

Each pod can process XML independently.

Do not introduce distributed locking unless the business use case explicitly requires exactly-once processing.

---

20. Idempotency

The flattening library itself should NOT invent business-level idempotency.

Instead expose enough information for the hosting application to implement it.

Example:

messageId
transactionId
endToEndId

The application layer can use these for:

database uniqueness
Kafka idempotency
deduplication

Do not use an in-memory "Set" for distributed deduplication.

---

21. Concurrency

Do not automatically create unlimited threads.

Use bounded concurrency at the application layer.

Example:

HTTP/Kafka/File input
        │
        ▼
Bounded Executor
        │
        ├── XML 1
        ├── XML 2
        ├── XML 3
        └── XML N

The concurrency limit must be configurable.

Virtual threads may be evaluated, but do NOT assume virtual threads automatically improve XML parsing throughput.

Benchmark:

Platform threads
vs
Virtual threads

before selecting the production strategy.

---

22. Backpressure

The library must support streaming consumption.

Example:

engine.flatten(input, record -> {
    output.write(record);
});

The producer must not outrun the consumer indefinitely.

If Kafka/database output is slow, the application must use bounded buffering.

Never use an unbounded queue.

